<!--
  Launcher icon, monochrome layer for themed icons. Original work,
  Copyright (C) 2026 Eyed.

  A separate drawing, not `@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground` pointed at a
  second time. The template did exactly that — background, foreground *and*
  monochrome all resolved to the same two files — and it is the usual mistake,
  because the foreground is designed against a known background and the
  monochrome layer is not.

  The mark is one tone already, so what changes here is only its size: themed
  icons sit on a solid system plate with no tile of their own, and a glyph
  tuned to fill a coloured tile looks oversized on one. The whole mark is
  inset 5% about (54,54) — ink spans x 31.2..76.8 and y 34.05..73.95, furthest
  on-path point 26.9 of the 33-unit safe radius. Every coordinate below is the
  foreground path times 0.95 about the centre, and nothing else;
  LauncherIconTest checks exactly that.

  The colour is `#FF000000` because the system re-tints this layer to the
  theme's foreground colour and only the alpha channel survives the trip.
  Black is what Google's own themed assets use, so a launcher that skipped the
  tint would still show a legible dark glyph on a light plate rather than
  nothing at all.

  This drawable is also the in-app brand glyph (the setup and empty-state
  screens tint it themselves), which is one more reason it is a single path
  with no colour of its own.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
